---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Privacy" description="Privacy Policy">
  <div class="prose prose-invert prose-slate mx-auto px-5 py-14 md:py-20">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>
      Last updated: October 20th, 2026.
    </p>
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy sets out how the <strong
        class="font-serif">ephira</strong
      > app uses and protects your personal data that you generate and store within the
      app.
    </p>
    <h2>Data Use</h2>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong> respects and celebrates your privacy.
      There is no collection of usage data or personal information, no ads, no spyware.
      <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong> stores encrypted data related to
      menstrual health <em>locally</em> on your device.
    </p>
    <p>This includes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>settings, including the app lock</li>
      <li>the days you log: flow, symptoms, moods, medications, birth control, intercourse and notes</li>
      <li>symptoms, moods and medications you add yourself</li>
      <li>the history of your period predictions</li>
      <li>pregnancy mode's start date, days and appointments</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The data is used to display statistics and apply fertility awareness
      rules. This data cannot be accessed by other apps.
    </p>
    <p>
      To delete your data, go to <strong>Settings › Data › Delete Data</strong>,
      type in <em>delete data</em> and press <strong>Delete Data</strong>. This
      deletes the days you log, the symptoms, moods and medications you add,
      and your settings, including the app lock. <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong> 1.5.0 keeps your pregnancy
      days and appointments, the history of your period predictions, scheduled
      reminders and any export files; uninstalling the app deletes those too.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong> encrypts your data with a key
      that never leaves your phone, so a backup holds nothing readable without
      that phone. Restoring a backup to the same iPhone brings back the data it
      held. The exceptions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If ephira cannot set up encryption, for example because your phone's
        secure key storage fails, it keeps your data unencrypted, without
        telling you, and tries again at each launch.
      </li>
      <li>
        On iPhone, a PDF exported with ephira 1.5.0 or earlier is kept where
        backups include it.
      </li>
      <li>
        A backup made before ephira began encrypting may still hold a readable
        copy until your phone replaces it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The encrypted database does not move to a new phone. To keep a copy when you change
      phones, export it first, though the export does not yet carry everything
      ephira stores (<a href="https://github.com/adulbrich/ephira/issues/298">issue 298</a>).
    </p>
    <h2>Permissions</h2>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong> includes an optional feature to enhance security 
      by enabling device biometrics, such as fingerprint or face recognition. This feature:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li> Uses your device's built-in biometric authentication functionality. </li>
      <li> Does not store or process biometric data within the app. All biometric data remains managed 
            by your device's operating system and is not accessible to <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong>. </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If your device's biometric system requires access to hardware components like the camera for setup or authentication 
      (e.g., for facial recognition), this is managed entirely by your device and not by <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you turn on notifications in Settings, <strong class="font-serif">ephira</strong>
      asks for permission to send reminders about your cycle. They are
      scheduled on your phone, and nothing is sent to a server. Their text, such
      as "Period Expected Tomorrow" or a note that your period is late, can show
      on your lock screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      Links to websites, such as the sources on cycle phases, open in a web
      browser.
    </p>
    <h2>Changes to this Privacy Policy</h2>
    <p>
      We may update our Privacy Policy from time to time. We will notify you of any changes by posting the new Privacy Policy on this page.
    </p>
    <h2>Contact Us</h2>
    <p>
      If you have any questions about this Privacy Policy, you can contact us:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        By email: <a href="mailto:ephira@capucity.be">ephira@capucity.be</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
